{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790888588,"nonce":"qAp0E0CZRqvXWiZ2uuVBozQs52Y=","title":"An Investigator cannot read the cluster, because no ToolServer can be configured","message":"**What is wrong.** ADR 0016 and 0053: cluster context arrives through an operator-run MCP server; the Investigator holds an explicit Tool allowlist with per-call timeout and result-size cap. No MCP client exists.\n\n**The demonstration.** No ToolServer table or MCP dependency.\n\n**Why it matters.** This is what makes Findings about the cluster rather than only oto's history — Kubernetes, VictoriaLogs and VictoriaMetrics MCP servers.\n\n**Where.** ToolServer config (URL, sealed credential, declared read-only); Tool discovery; allowlist with no wildcards; per-call timeout and size cap recorded on the Step; results redacted with the ingest redaction rules.\n\n**Scope.** medium.\n\n**Done when.** An Investigator calls an allowlisted read-only Tool and the Step records it; a call outside the allowlist is refused and recorded; timeouts and truncation are recorded and the run continues; oto stores no cluster credential beyond the ToolServer's own access token.\n\n**Blocked by.** `180a525`\n\n**Mode.** AFK — can be implemented and merged without a human decision. Source: ADR 0052/0053/0054 design session, 2026-10-02.","files":null}]}